== Typology ==
Alamblak is a [[Polysynthetic language|polysynthetic]] language. It is highly [[Agglutination|agglutinative]] with some [[Fusional language|fusional]] elements. It exhibits several linguistic features that generally indicate polysynthesis in a Papuan-language context.<ref>{{harvp|Foley|2017}}</ref> Some of these include [[Polypersonal agreement|polypersonalism]] (marking of multiple arguments on verbs) and heavy [[Head-marking language|head-marking]]. Its basic [[word order]] is [[Subject–object–verb word order|subject–object–verb]], and several other morphosyntactic features generally associated with SOV languages are also exhibited in Alamblak. Specifically, [[subordinate clauses]] precede independent clauses (e.g., [[relative clause]]s precede the head), [[Grammatical case|case]] relators follow the noun (as enclitics or suffixes), and the interrogative element is not [[Wh-movement|fronted]] in a clause, but remains ''in situ''. Examples of these [[Linguistic typology|typological]] features can be seen below. Examples are from Bruce (1984).

{{interlinear|indent=3
|top=<u>OV Word Order</u>
|t­ɨndht pɨthë-rhwa-an-t
|legend tell-FUT-1SG-3S.F
|‘I will tell you a story’}}

{{interlinear|indent=3
|top=<u>Relative Clause</u>
|me-t hoitwa kuñ-t
|woman-3S.F sleeps house-3S.F
|‘a house in which a woman sleeps’}}

{{interlinear|indent=3
|top=<u>Case marking</u>
|wai kuñ-kimb-ko
|go house-side-AL
|‘Go to the side of the house’}}

